WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Two sentences, same verb:
«Je sais qu'il vient.» «Je

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.900
veux qu'il vienne.» After 'know':

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.900
«vient». After 'want': «vienne».

4
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.470
A different spelling,
and a different sound.

5
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.040
English hides it.

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.150
'I know he's coming.'
'I want him to come.'

7
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.380
French makes you pick a verb form.

8
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.140
And 'want' isn't alone.

9
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.570
Some everyday phrases change the verb.

10
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.670
Their look-alikes never do.

11
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.390
So what decides when
French changes the verb,

12
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.840
and when it doesn't?

13
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.260
In this mission, you'll press
Mission D1's four buttons

14
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.120
and watch the verb react.

15
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.710
Then you'll sort look-alike phrases
onto Mission D3's two shelves.

16
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.010
Your mission: work out when
French changes the verb,

17
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.390
and when it leaves it alone.
